    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-9698",
      "problem": "It is known that there is a linear correlation between the variables $x$ and $y.$ Given that the correlation coefficient is $\\rho\\approx -0.85,$ sketch a possible scatter plot that corresponds to this situation.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ satisfies the inequality\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho = 1$ indicates a perfect positive correlation, and $\\rho = -1$ indicates a perfect negative correlation.\n\nTherefore, since $\\rho \\approx -0.85,$ there is a *strong negative* linear correlation in the data.\n\n- The *negative* linear correlation means that the data points should be scattered along a line with a *negative* slope.\n\n- Since the correlation is *strong*, most of the points should lie *close* to the trend line.\n\nTherefore, this situation could correspond to the following scatter plot:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14226558/assets/ad8ff5e49649b5e788b83eb28fa26afae61072d5e63026631a9cd1891be14a0c.png)",
